            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, the air leakage from your home is quite reasonable, however there are a few key areas that should be addressed. There is a combustion air supply for the woodstove that runs through the lower tank room &amp; then out through the side wall. This pipe has come apart and it now allowing cold air to enter the house or warm air to be sucked out of the house depending on the wind direction. This combustion air pipe should be replaced with a rigid pipe (like dryer vent pipe) and have the joints sealed with metal tape.



Overall - both doors are reasonable and can likely just be maintained by ensuring the door locks/latches + weatherstripping are all in good working order.



Both bathroom exhaust fans appear to allow heat loss even when the fans are not operating. You may want to consider upgrading these fans to new Energy Star models that have good butterfly dampers, use less electricity &amp; operate alot quieter.



Some other smaller air leakage in your house is from around &amp; through many of the electrical outlets &amp; wall switches located on exterior walls. For these electrical outlets, you can install the specially designed foam gaskets behind the cover plates.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 211&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of both the upper house and the lower heated tank room are 2x6 construction with R20 fibreglass batt insulation. For your longer term planning, I have modeled the expected energy savings if you were to add 3" (R15) of rigid foam insulation to the exterior of these walls in conjunction with a future exterior siding upgrade.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls of the house up to a minimum R35 insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,078&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;24.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</mainWalls>
                <windows />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 223 litre electric hot water tank, and the cost of electricity in Norman Wells is very expensive. In fact, electricity in Norman Wells is generated primarily using diesel and the efficiency of the diesel generators is often less than if you used the oil directly to create the hot water. I did see in this house that it used to have a gas hot water heater (that was vented up a chimney), however it was replaced with an electric hot water tank in conjunction with the conversion away from natural gas in Norman Wells a few years ago.



The Arctic Energy Alliance is starting to see a variety of higher efficiency oil hot water heaters being installed in all regions of the Northwest Territories (most are direct vent through a side wall, rather than up a chimney). Although we do not recommend or endorse any manufacturers or products, I will mention a unit that we are seeing more NWT contractors starting to install - the Toyotomi OM-122HW (and I saw some in Norman Wells). We are advised by Natural Resources Canada that this unit is not currently Canadian ENERGY STAR® rated, however they indicate it has an Energy Factor of 0.682.



The AEA adjudicates the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP) and the current guidelines indicate that an instantaneous on-demand oil hot water heater must have a minimum 0.68 EF and be Canadian ENERGY STAR® rated. As such, the Toyotomi OM-122HW is not eligible for the $1,250 EEIP rebate ........... however we are currently reviewing this with the GNWT - so please call me or check our website for an update. 





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your hot electric hot water tank with an instantaneous oil-fired water heater with a 0.682 EF, there would be a small increase in overall energy use, however the cost difference between oil &amp; electricity indicates that you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save up to $ 969&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. As mentioned above, this upgrade does not currently qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Furnace Upgrades&lt;/b&gt;



Your house has 2 older direct vent oil furnaces - one that heats the front half of the house and the other heats the back half of the house - and each furnace has a few supply pipes to push hot air into the lower tank room. Both furnaces are rated at 83% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE), and each one has a 4" fresh air supply pipe connected to the top to bring fresh air into the house (whenever an exhaust fan is operating), and the furnace fan will then evenly distribute the fresh air throughout the house.



For your information, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified, direct vent oil furnaces with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your current oil furnaces with a higher-efficiency 87% AFUE direct vent model, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 210&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. To qualify for the &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, oil furnaces need to be a minimum 92% AFUE ........... however I do not currently see any mechanical contractors in your region installing this type of furnace. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Using the Wood Stove&lt;/b&gt;



You had asked about the cost savings associated with using the wood stove to offset oil furnace use. As there are two furnaces (one for the front half of the house &amp; one for the rear half), and the woodstove is located in the front half - I have modeled the projected energy savings of using the wood stove to offset 50% of the heating demand of the front half of the house.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

Using the woodstove to offset 50% of the heating requirements of the front half of the house could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,617&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs (net of oil savings less cost of self-harvested wood @ an assumed rate of $100 per cord - to recognize expenses such as the chainsaw, gas, oil, transportation ........ but your time is free !!)



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



As discussed while I was at your house, the Arctic Energy Alliance always recommends homeowners complete all energy conservation (eg: LED Bulbs, timer switches, etc.) &amp; energy efficiency (eg: heating &amp; hot water systems) measures before investing in renewable energy generation systems.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the front Southwest facing roof of your house, flush mounted on the roof, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save about $ 894&lt;/b&gt; per year in electricity costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 5,000 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system: 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $5,000 per year in a thermal electricity community. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two toilets and both are older models, and you should consider replacing them with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



This house has multiple components comprising the ventilation design:



1. A central ventilation fan in the hallway outside the bathroom, that is operated by a humidistat (this was in the off position, and is normally only used in the summer for additional ventilation of hot, humid air from within the house).



2. Both bathrooms have an exhaust fan, operated by a regular switch. An easy upgrade is to install timer switches (5 -10-15-20 minutes) so the fans will be operated long enough to exhaust odours &amp; humidity, however cannot be accidentally left on all day, wasting large amounts of heated air from your house.



3. The kitchen has a range hood fan that appears to be working well. When is is time to replace it, install Energy Star rated fan that has a good butterfly damper (to prevent excess heat loss when the fan is not operating), uses less electricity and has a low noise (sone) level. 



4. The clothes dryer is vented to the outside. There was a buildup of clothes lint in the exhaust vent (at the outside of the house), so I cleaned it out for you while I was there.



5.  Both furnaces have a 4" fresh air pipe that feeds into the top of the furnace, and this "make-up" are helps to replace the air being exhausted byt the exhaust fans outlined above. Also - each time the furnace operates, it draws in a bit of outside air to help maintain a healthy indoor air quality.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood, both bathroom fans, the central ventilation fan in the hallway and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 6.0 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could cause combustion products to be drawn into your home. Both furnaces are sealed combustion (direct vent) models so they are not at risk. The woodstove has a dedicated combustion air side from below, however that pipe is broken in the lower tank room area &amp; should be fixed. A wood stove can also backdraft into the house each time you open the door to load more wood .......... so you should ensure exhaust fans are off when you load more wood.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be functioning smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date (normally every 10 years).



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Norman Wells&lt;/b&gt;



The following Nov. 1, 2017 values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Norman Wells:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh up to the TPSP threshold, $0.54 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.52 per litre 

Wood - $472.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.14 per bag



